-- A RULE THAT READS ITS OWN GOAL (MS12) — the acceptance test for a core
-- pattern in a rule head.
--
-- `by-induction` in the shipped base takes no arguments. It matches that its
-- goal is a universal, names the three parts of it, introduces the binder and
-- inducts on that parameter:
--
--   rule by-induction at ? _ : core`∀ (${x} : ${T}) -> ${E}` :-
--     do intro x
--        ; v = ident-term x
--        ; call induction-core v
--
-- Until a pattern could stand over a core term, none of that could be written.
-- `goal` hands a rule a core term and every destructor in the vocabulary
-- answers a surface one, so a nullary rule could ask the yes-or-no questions —
-- is the focus a hole, is its type a ∀ — and learn nothing further. It could
-- recognise the shape of its goal and still not name the parts it would have
-- to hand to `induction-core`.
--
-- WHAT TO COMPARE IT WITH: `SplitAtAHole.thena.script`, beside this file,
-- makes the same kind of split by hand. There the author writes
--
--   attack ; intro ; intro ; intro ; induction-core core`b`
--
-- and names the scrutinee themselves, in a term they wrote out. Here nothing
-- is written down: the rule is handed no argument and the subject of the split
-- is the binder its own head bound.

data Nat : Type₀ where { zero : Nat ; succ : Nat -> Nat }
open Nat

-- IT IS OFFERED TO THE SEARCH, which is the half the proof below does not
-- show. `by-induction` is nullary, so it is a candidate wherever a bare `step`
-- looks, and it sits between `intro` and `attack` in the base: introducing is
-- the strictly less committal move and is this rule's own first instruction,
-- so `step` takes that and one `retry` reaches the case split.
:theorem reachedBySearch : ∀ (n : Nat) -> Eq {0} Nat n n
attack
step
:choices
retry
:abandon

-- AND THE PROOF, with the rule called by name. One hole per constructor, each
-- case's binders introduced and its equations already discharged — the same
-- thing `induction-core` hands an author who names the scrutinee, reached
-- without naming one.
--
-- `qed` is the judge: it runs the kernel over the finished development, so a
-- goal the rule shaped wrongly could not be proved past.
:theorem natRefl : ∀ (n : Nat) -> Eq {0} Nat n n
attack
by-induction

-- THE CASES ARE REFINED, which is what makes the two of them different proofs:
-- `n` is a definition inside each one — `zero` here, `succ x` below — so the
-- goal names the constructor rather than the parameter.
goto-named "zeroCase1"
try-core core`refl {0} Nat zero`
solve
goto-named "zeroCase"
solve

-- The `succ` case carries its induction hypothesis, `x1 : Eq {0} Nat x x`, and
-- this theorem does not need it: `refl` closes both cases. It is in scope all
-- the same, which is what `induction-core` gives that `case-core` does not.
goto-named "succCase1"
try-core core`refl {0} Nat (succ x)`
solve
goto-named "succCase"
solve

goto-named "zeroMethod"
solve
goto-named "succMethod"
solve
goto-named "natRefl1"
solve
goto-root
solve
qed
